```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>")
options(width = 84)
```

EAVS is a census of election jurisdictions, not a sample, so there are no survey
weights and no sampling error to report. What there is instead is item
nonresponse, unevenly spread across roughly 6,460 independent jurisdictions. A
state total is the sum of whoever answered, and the useful question is always
how much of the state that was.

```{r load}
library(tidyeavs)

panel <- eavs_load(2024, quiet = TRUE)
```

## Why a value is absent

EAVS does not just leave cells empty. It distinguishes "does not apply" (`-88`),
"data not available" (`-99`), and a valid skip (`-77`), and those mean opposite
things: the first says nothing is missing, the second says something is. Loading
decodes all of them to `NA`, which is convenient and throws the distinction
away. `eavs_missing_status()` recovers it from the raw file:

```{r status}
raw <- eavs_read(2024, quiet = TRUE)
status <- eavs_harmonize(eavs_missing_status(raw), year = 2024)
table(status$prov_rejected)
```

A thousand jurisdictions did not have provisional ballots to reject. Counting
them as a gap would be wrong, and the difference is not small:

```{r coverage-compare}
without <- eavs_aggregate(panel)
with    <- eavs_aggregate(panel, status = status)

round(c(
  collapsed = mean(without$coverage[without$concept == "prov_rejected"], na.rm = TRUE),
  reason_aware = mean(with$coverage[with$concept == "prov_rejected"], na.rm = TRUE)
), 3)
```

Pass `status` whenever you care about coverage. Without it every absence lands in
`n_missing` and coverage reads as a lower bound; the `coverage_exact` column
records which of the two you are looking at.

## The five buckets

```{r buckets}
cols <- c("n_reported", "n_missing", "n_blank", "n_not_applicable",
          "n_not_collected", "n_total")
subset(with, concept == "prov_rejected" & state_abbr %in% c("ME", "AR", "GA"),
       select = c("state_abbr", cols))
```

Maine is the clearest illustration: nearly every one of its municipalities said
the item does not apply, so almost nothing is missing even though almost nothing
was reported. Arkansas shows all three kinds of absence at once.

`n_reported` gave a number. `n_missing` are the genuine gaps. `n_blank` left the
cell empty, which the file does not explain, so those are counted apart rather
than assumed either way. `n_not_applicable` said the item did not pertain.
`n_not_collected` were never asked, because the concept was not on that year's
questionnaire. The five always sum to `n_total`.

`coverage` is `n_reported` over the first three: the share of jurisdictions that
could have answered and did. Blanks count as gaps, which keeps coverage a lower
bound wherever they are common.

## Jurisdictions are not the same size

Coverage counts jurisdictions, and jurisdictions are wildly unequal. A rollup
missing Los Angeles County is not comparable to one missing a Wisconsin town of
forty voters. `coverage_reg` weights the same ratio by each jurisdiction's
registered and eligible voters:

```{r weighted}
cured <- subset(with, concept == "ballots_cured" & state_abbr %in% c("AR", "OH", "CA"),
                select = c("state_abbr", "n_reported", "n_total",
                           "coverage", "coverage_reg"))
transform(cured, coverage = round(coverage, 3), coverage_reg = round(coverage_reg, 3))
```

Arkansas reported curing counts from 6 of its 75 jurisdictions, which is 8% of
them but 16% of its registered voters: the reporters skew large, as they usually
do. Since `value` is a sum of counts, the weighted figure is generally the better
guide to how much of the total you actually have.

This weights the diagnostic only. No value is ever reweighted, because EAVS has
no weights to apply. And registration concepts weighted by registration are
mildly circular, which is documented rather than special-cased.

## What a total is, and is not

Every EAVS item is a nonnegative count, so a total computed with `na.rm = TRUE`
over incomplete reporting is a **lower bound**. That is arithmetic, not
interpretation. The package will not scale a total up to compensate, because
doing so would require assuming the non-reporters look like the reporters, and
that assumption is exactly what missingness this uneven makes unsafe.

Two habits follow. Report coverage next to any total you publish. And when
coverage is low, prefer a rate over a level, since a rate over the common subset
(see `vignette("getting-started")`) is far less sensitive to who is absent than a
sum is.

## Blanks, and one specific case

The ambiguous bucket has a concentration worth knowing about. In 2016 nearly all
blanks in the shipped concepts are Maine, whose municipalities leave the UOCAVA
items to a statewide row. The EAC codes those same rows "does not apply" in later
cycles, so the 2016 blanks are almost certainly skips rather than nonresponse:

```{r maine}
p16 <- eavs_load(2016, quiet = TRUE)
s16 <- eavs_harmonize(eavs_missing_status(eavs_read(2016, quiet = TRUE)), year = 2016)
a16 <- eavs_aggregate(p16, status = s16)
subset(a16, state_abbr == "ME" & concept == "uocava_counted",
       select = c("n_reported", "n_missing", "n_blank", "coverage"))
```

They are counted as blanks rather than reinterpreted, because the file does not
say and this package does not guess. If you decide they are skips, moving them
out of the denominator is your call to make and one line to do.
